  "customfield_10187": null,
  "description": "<p>When the batch modify parser encounters an inline xml datastream that looks like:</p>\n\n<p>&lt;a:element xmlns:a=\"urn:example:a\"&gt;<br/>\n  &lt;b:subelement xmlns:b=\"urn:example:b\"&gt;<br/>\n  &lt;/b:subelement&gt;<br/>\n&lt;/a:element&gt;</p>\n\n<p>...the output it sends to Fedora is not well-formed.  In particular, the namespace declarations are moved to the root, but still present in the sub-elements, minus the attribute name, like so:</p>\n\n<p>&lt;a:element xmlns:a=\"urn:example:a\" xmlns:b=\"urn:example:b\"&gt;<br/>\n  &lt;b:subelement =\"urn:example:b\"&gt;<br/>\n  &lt;/b:subelement&gt;<br/>\n&lt;/a:element&gt;</p>\n\n<p>Ideally, the output that the batch modify utility sends to Fedora should be exactly the XML that occurs within the xmlContent element.</p>\n\n<p>This was originally reported by Deborah Kaplan at Tufts on May 27th, 2008.  It was thought to have been fixed by bugfix #1838750, but on July 11th, Deborah reported that wasn't the case and I verified.</p>\n\n<p>A fix should ensure any well-formed inline XML, regardless of how namespaces are declared, arrives to Fedora in the same way it was given in the batch modify directives file &#8211; without changing where namespaces are declared.</p>\n\n<p>This bug needs to be fixed in trunk and maintenance-2.2, with appropriate tests.</p>",
